# Black Umbrella Group — Architecture Decision Register

```
DOCUMENT:         Architecture Decision Register
VERSION:          V1
STATUS:           Active
OWNER:            Black Umbrella Governance
MAINTAINED BY:    AIOS (Administrative)
DATE ESTABLISHED: 2026-07-03
PROVISIONAL NOTE: Permanent classification and repository location
                  pending the Governance Audit. Authority is not
                  affected by provisional location.
```

---

## Register Purpose

This register is the authoritative record of all Architecture Decisions produced under the Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard.

The Register owns: permanent identifiers, governance status, Proposal references, supersession relationships, and registration metadata.

The Register records governance. It does not own governance. Governance belongs to Black Umbrella Governance.

---

## Initial Registration

The following 23 Architecture Decisions were registered on 2026-07-03 following Founder Approval of the Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal.

This is the initial population of the Architecture Decision Register and represents the bootstrap of Black Umbrella's permanent governance record.

---

# Registered Architecture Decisions

---

## AD-001 — Architecture Decisions Are Permanent Governance Records

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-001 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Architecture Decisions are permanent records of Black Umbrella governance. They persist indefinitely regardless of governance status changes, Office dissolution, platform migration, or the passage of time. They are never deleted.

**Context**
Black Umbrella required a formal mechanism to preserve approved governance decisions across time, independently of the conversations, tools, and personnel that produced them. Governance decisions needed to outlast any individual engagement, tool, or platform.

**Rationale**
Permanence ensures organisational knowledge is preserved across leadership changes, platform migrations, and the passage of time. Without permanence, governance becomes volatile — dependent on whether conversations and documents happen to survive. Permanence is the foundational property that makes Architecture Decisions a trustworthy long-term governance record.

**Impact**
All governance artefacts, all Offices, AIOS, repository, implementation.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-002 — Architecture Decisions Record Approved Decisions Only

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-002 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Architecture Decisions record approved governance decisions only. They do not record discussions, Proposals, alternatives considered, or implementation activity.

**Context**
Black Umbrella governance involves lengthy discussions, Proposal documents, and implementation records. A permanent record type was needed that captured only the approved outcome — not the journey to it. Without this distinction, permanent governance records would be indistinguishable from working documents.

**Rationale**
Mixing decision records with discussion or implementation history creates ambiguity about what was actually decided. A reader five years later should find a clear, unambiguous record of what was approved. Discussions and Proposals remain as separate historical evidence. Architecture Decisions carry only the approved outcome.

**Impact**
Governance records, Offices, AIOS, future governance consumers.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-003 — One Architecture Decision Records One Decision (Atomicity)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-003 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Each Architecture Decision records exactly one approved governance decision. Multiple independent decisions must not be combined into a single Architecture Decision.

**Context**
During governance design, the risk of bundling related decisions was identified. Bundled decisions create governance ambiguity: if one part of the decision is superseded, the entire record becomes inconsistent, making it unclear which parts of the governance remain in force.

**Rationale**
Atomicity ensures each Architecture Decision can be independently superseded, consumed, or referenced without affecting unrelated decisions. It also prevents governance scope creep within individual records. Atomicity is the property that keeps Architecture Decisions precise and durable over time.

**Impact**
Governance, Proposals, AD Register, AIOS.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-004 — Architecture Decisions Must Be Self-Contained

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-004 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Every Architecture Decision must be understandable without recovering the Proposal, discussion, or context that produced it. References exist for traceability but must never be required for comprehension.

**Context**
Black Umbrella governance discussions produce extensive context that may not remain accessible years later. An Architecture Decision needed to outlast that context so that future Offices, AIOS, and governance audits could understand approved decisions independently.

**Rationale**
Self-containedness makes Architecture Decisions durable governance records. It ensures AIOS and future Offices can consume decisions reliably without needing access to historical conversations or archived Proposals. References support navigation. They do not substitute for understanding.

**Impact**
Governance records, Offices, AIOS, future governance consumers.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-005 — Approved Architecture Decisions Are Immutable

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-005 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Approved Architecture Decisions may not be modified after registration. Future governance creates new Architecture Decisions and supersedes previous ones. It does not rewrite historical governance records.

**Context**
Once Founder Approval is granted, an Architecture Decision represents a historical governance event. Black Umbrella needed a principle that prevented historical governance from being silently amended, which would undermine the integrity of the governance record.

**Rationale**
Immutability preserves governance integrity. It ensures that anyone reading an Architecture Decision knows they are reading exactly what was approved at the time of approval — not a retrospectively edited version. If organisational direction changes, the correct response is a new Proposal, new Approval, a new Architecture Decision, and a supersession relationship. History remains intact.

**Impact**
All governance records, AD Register, AIOS, governance audits.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-006 — Proposals and Architecture Decisions Are Separate Governance Artefacts

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-006 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
A Proposal and an Architecture Decision are distinct governance artefacts with different purposes and different lifecycles. A Proposal recommends and persuades. An Architecture Decision records and preserves. Neither replaces the other.

**Context**
During governance design, a risk was identified that Proposals and Architecture Decisions might be conflated — either by treating the Proposal as the permanent record or by duplicating Proposal content inside Architecture Decisions. Both would undermine governance clarity.

**Rationale**
Keeping them separate ensures each artefact has a single clear purpose. The Proposal is contextual and historical. The Architecture Decision is authoritative and permanent. Conflating them would either make governance too verbose or cause historical context to be lost.

**Impact**
Governance, Offices, Architecture & Governance, Implementation Lead, AIOS, repository.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-007 — One Proposal May Generate Multiple Architecture Decisions

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-007 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
A single Proposal may produce one or more Architecture Decisions. Each Architecture Decision produced must remain atomic. The Proposal acts as the governance container for a set of related proposed decisions.

**Context**
Governance work often involves clusters of related decisions that should be reviewed and approved together. Requiring one Proposal per decision would fragment governance and obscure the contextual relationship between related decisions.

**Rationale**
Allowing one Proposal to produce multiple ADs enables coherent governance packages while preserving atomicity at the Architecture Decision level. The Proposal provides the shared context; each AD records one discrete decision from within that context.

**Impact**
Governance, Proposals, AD Register, AIOS, Offices.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-008 — Architecture Decisions Are Embedded in the Proposal Before Founder Approval

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-008 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Every Proposal shall contain a dedicated section defining the Architecture Decisions it intends to create, prior to submission for Founder Approval. No Architecture Decision may be introduced after Founder Approval.

**Context**
Black Umbrella required a governance model where the Founder approves both the Proposal context and the exact Architecture Decision records it will produce. This required Architecture Decisions to be authored and embedded during Stage 2 — not created afterwards.

**Rationale**
Embedding Architecture Decisions before approval ensures the Founder's approval covers the exact governance records that will enter the register. Post-approval AD creation would mean the register contains decisions that were never explicitly approved. The embedding rule prevents this gap.

**Impact**
Proposals, Governance, Architecture & Governance, Implementation Lead, Founder Approval, AIOS.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-009 — Founder Approval Includes Approval of Every Embedded Architecture Decision

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-009 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
When the Founder approves a Proposal, that approval includes explicit approval of every Architecture Decision embedded within that Proposal.

**Context**
A potential governance gap was identified: without explicit coverage, registration could produce ADs that were technically never individually approved — only the Proposal surrounding them was approved.

**Rationale**
Making Founder Approval cover both the Proposal and its embedded ADs closes the governance gap. There is no separate approval event for individual ADs. The Proposal approval is the governance event. This keeps the governance process clean and prevents a proliferation of approval checkpoints.

**Impact**
Governance, Founder Approval, AD Register, AIOS, Proposals.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-010 — Architecture Decision Registration Is Administrative, Not Governance

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-010 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Registration of Architecture Decisions is an administrative activity. It records approved governance. It does not create governance, interpret governance, or perform any governance act. The governance event is Founder Approval.

**Context**
Registration occurs after Founder Approval. Without a clear categorisation, registration could be misread as a governance checkpoint — giving the registration process authority it was never intended to have.

**Rationale**
Separating administration from governance is foundational to Black Umbrella's governance model: Governance decides. Administration records. If registration were treated as governance, it would introduce an unauthorised decision point between Founder Approval and the AD Register. Categorising it as administrative removes that ambiguity.

**Impact**
Governance, AD Register, AIOS, registration process.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-011 — Architecture Decision Identifiers Are Assigned by the AD Register

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-011 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Permanent Architecture Decision identifiers are assigned by the Architecture Decision Register during registration. They are not assigned by the Proposal, the Founder, or any governance process.

**Context**
Proposals contain Architecture Decisions before Founder Approval using temporary references. A decision was required on when and how permanent identifiers would be assigned.

**Rationale**
Assigning identifiers during registration keeps identifier assignment administrative. The Founder approves decisions, not numbers. Pre-assigning identifiers in the Proposal would require coordination before approval and would create gaps in the identifier sequence if Proposals were rejected. Register-assigned identifiers ensure a clean, sequential, gap-free record.

**Impact**
AD Register, Proposals, AIOS, governance consumers.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-012 — The AD Register Is the Authoritative Source of Architecture Decision Identifiers

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-012 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
The Architecture Decision Register is the single authoritative source of Architecture Decision identifiers. No other document, system, or process may assign or override permanent AD identifiers.

**Context**
Multiple systems and Offices will reference Architecture Decisions over time. Without a single authoritative source, identifier conflicts and ambiguity could develop across different references and tools.

**Rationale**
Single-source identifier authority prevents duplication and ambiguity. Any system, Office, or AIOS process that needs to reference an Architecture Decision does so by consulting the Register. The Register's identifiers are definitive.

**Impact**
AD Register, AIOS, Offices, implementation, repository.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-013 — Approved Architecture Decisions Are Owned by Black Umbrella Governance

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-013 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Following registration, ownership of an Architecture Decision transfers to Black Umbrella Governance. No Office retains ownership of an approved Architecture Decision. Ownership does not return to any Office after registration.

**Context**
Architecture Decisions are produced by Offices through Proposals. Without a clear ownership transfer, ambiguity could develop about which Office has authority over a registered Architecture Decision — particularly after Offices are restructured, merged, or dissolved.

**Rationale**
Governance ownership prevents Offices from treating Architecture Decisions as proprietary. It ensures any Office may reference and consume ADs regardless of which Office originally produced them. It also ensures status changes require governance rather than Office action.

**Impact**
All Offices, Governance, AD Register, AIOS.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-014 — Only Governance May Alter the Status of an Architecture Decision

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-014 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
The status of a registered Architecture Decision may only be changed through a formal governance event — specifically, a new Proposal progressed through the Work Lifecycle with Founder Approval. No Office, administrative process, or implementation activity may independently alter an Architecture Decision's status.

**Context**
After registration, Architecture Decisions carry governance authority. Without restricting status changes to governance events, Offices or implementation processes could retire, supersede, or cancel ADs without appropriate authority.

**Rationale**
Restricting status changes to governance events preserves governance integrity. Every status change — supersession, retirement, cancellation — carries the same level of authority as the original approval. Implementation cannot overwrite governance. Administration cannot overwrite governance.

**Impact**
Governance, Offices, AD Register, AIOS, implementation.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-015 — Approved Architecture Decisions Enter the Approved Decision Queue Following Registration

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-015 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Immediately following registration, every approved Architecture Decision enters the Approved Decision Queue. The queue represents governance approval awaiting execution authorisation.

**Context**
Black Umbrella governance separates Founder Approval (authorising what is correct governance) from Founder Execute (authorising when implementation may begin). A mechanism was needed to hold approved decisions in a visible, actionable state between these two events.

**Rationale**
The queue ensures approved decisions remain visible and actionable without being confused with implementation-in-progress. An AD may remain in the queue indefinitely without affecting its governance validity. Execution timing is a separate governance decision from decision approval.

**Impact**
Governance, Approved Decision Queue, Offices, AIOS, Founder Execute process.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-016 — The Approved Decision Queue Contains Architecture Decisions, Not Proposals

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-016 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
The Approved Decision Queue contains approved Architecture Decisions. It does not contain Proposals, discussions, implementation tasks, or any other governance artefact.

**Context**
During governance design, the Work Lifecycle Standard was identified as using "Approved Work Queue" language that implied Proposals might enter the queue. Explicit correction was required to establish that only Architecture Decisions enter the queue.

**Rationale**
Proposals are governance history. Architecture Decisions are governance authority. The queue should contain what has been decided — not the argument for deciding it. Placing ADs rather than Proposals in the queue ensures the queue represents actionable governance.

**Impact**
Approved Decision Queue, Work Lifecycle Standard (pending amendment), AIOS, Offices.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-017 — Governed Implementation Maintains Bidirectional Traceability

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-017 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Every governed implementation shall maintain complete bidirectional traceability through the following chain: Repository Change → Operating Area → Architecture Decision → Proposal → Operating Standard → Founder Approval.

**Context**
Black Umbrella needed a governance model where any implementation could be traced back to the authority that approved it, and where any governance decision could be traced forward to its implementation outcome.

**Rationale**
Bidirectional traceability enables governance audits, implementation validation, and historical analysis. It ensures no implementation exists without governance authority, and every approved decision can be verified as implemented or tracked as pending. It also enables AIOS to navigate governance relationships reliably.

**Impact**
Repository, Offices, Implementation, AD Register, AIOS, governance audits.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-018 — AIOS Administers Architecture Decisions Without Interpreting Governance

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-018 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
AIOS may retrieve, register, validate, route, and reference Architecture Decisions as authorised administrative activities. AIOS may never interpret, redefine, create, supersede, or bypass governance through any action.

**Context**
AIOS is the operational intelligence layer of Black Umbrella. As it gains access to approved Architecture Decisions, a clear boundary was needed to prevent AIOS from treating its administrative access as governance authority.

**Rationale**
AIOS automates administration and implementation — not governance. If AIOS were permitted to interpret governance, it could silently alter the meaning of approved decisions through inference, summarisation, or contextual application. Governance must remain human-authorised and Founder-approved. AIOS serves governance; it does not make it.

**Impact**
AIOS, Governance, AD Register, all Offices.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-019 — Architecture Decisions Are Proposed by Offices

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-019 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Architecture Decisions originate through Proposals produced by Offices. No Architecture Decision may be created outside the Proposal and Work Lifecycle process.

**Context**
Black Umbrella required a clear entry point for Architecture Decision creation that preserved governance authority. Without specifying that Offices propose through the Work Lifecycle, informal or administrative AD creation could occur.

**Rationale**
Anchoring AD creation in the Office-to-Proposal-to-Lifecycle pathway ensures every Architecture Decision has a traceable governance origin. No AD may appear in the register without having been proposed through an authorised Proposal, reviewed through the Work Lifecycle, and approved by the Founder.

**Impact**
Offices, Governance, Work Lifecycle, Proposals, AD Register.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-020 — Architecture Decisions Are Approved by the Founder

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-020 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Founder Approval is the exclusive governance authority for Architecture Decisions. No Architecture Decision may be registered without Founder Approval of the Proposal containing it.

**Context**
Black Umbrella's governance model concentrates final approval authority with the Founder to ensure accountability and consistency. During governance design, it was confirmed that this authority should not be delegated.

**Rationale**
Founder Approval as the exclusive approval authority ensures governance cannot be self-approved by Offices or approved by AIOS. All Architecture Decisions carry the same level of governance authority because they all trace back to the same approval source. This creates a consistent, trustworthy governance record.

**Impact**
Founder, Governance, Offices, Work Lifecycle, AIOS.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-021 — Registered Architecture Decisions Are Permanently Owned by Black Umbrella Governance

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-021 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Upon registration, an Architecture Decision becomes the permanent property of Black Umbrella Governance. Ownership does not remain with the originating Office and does not transfer to any subsequent Office, regardless of organisational restructuring.

**Context**
Offices may change, be restructured, or be dissolved over time. If Architecture Decisions remained the property of their originating Office, governance records could become orphaned or subject to disputed ownership.

**Rationale**
Institutional ownership — rather than Office ownership — ensures Architecture Decisions survive organisational change. The originating Office is the author; Black Umbrella Governance is the permanent owner. This is analogous to a company owning board resolutions rather than the department that proposed them. Ownership does not mean unchangeable — it means only the governance process may change the status of an AD.

**Impact**
Offices, Governance, AD Register, future governance restructuring.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-022 — Approved Architecture Decisions May Be Consumed by Any Authorised Office

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-022 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Any Office authorised to operate within Black Umbrella may reference, consume, and implement approved Architecture Decisions. No Office requires permission from the originating Office to consume an AD.

**Context**
If Architecture Decisions were treated as the property of their originating Office, other Offices might need to request access or permission to act on relevant decisions. This would fragment governance and slow implementation.

**Rationale**
Universal consumption rights — subject to Office authorisation — ensure approved Architecture Decisions are accessible to any Office that needs them. This makes governance a shared organisational resource rather than an Office-held asset. Implementation proceeds wherever it is authorised, regardless of which Office proposed the original decision.

**Impact**
All Offices, Governance, AIOS, Implementation.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

---

## AD-023 — Every Architecture Decision Shall Contain Mandatory Governance Sections

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Identifier** | AD-023 |
| **Governance Status** | Approved |
| **Approved** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Registered** | 2026-07-03 |
| **Initiative** | INI-003 |

**Decision Statement**
Every registered Architecture Decision shall contain, at minimum, the mandatory sections defined by the Architecture Decision Standard: Identifier, Title, Decision Statement, Context, Rationale, Impact, Governance Status, and References.

**Context**
For Architecture Decisions to be self-contained, permanently understandable, and consistently structured, a minimum section standard was required. Without it, different Offices or time periods could produce incompatible AD formats.

**Rationale**
Mandatory sections ensure every Architecture Decision answers the four fundamental questions: what was approved, why it was approved, what it affects, and what its current status is. Structural consistency also enables AIOS to reliably parse and consume Architecture Decisions without bespoke handling per artefact.

**Impact**
All Architecture Decisions, Architecture & Governance, Implementation Lead, AIOS, AD Register.

**References**
- Originating Proposal: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard Proposal (2026-07-03)
- Governing Standard: Black Umbrella Architecture Decision Standard

