## AD-037

**IDENTIFIER:** AD-037
**TITLE:** Operating Areas Are Bounded Execution Contexts Instantiated by Commitments
**STATUS:** Approved — Awaiting Execute
**REGISTERED:** 2026-07-03
**INITIATIVE:** INI-003
**OWNER:** Black Umbrella Governance
**SOURCE PROPOSAL:** OPERATING_AREA_STANDARD_PROPOSAL_2026-07-03_V1.md
**TEMPORARY REFERENCE:** AD-A

### Decision Statement

An Operating Area is a bounded execution context instantiated by a Commitment to fulfil one coherent operational purpose under a defined execution contract. Operating Areas exist only as instantiated execution contexts within a Commitment.

### Context

The Commitment Standard established that Commitments own work and instantiate Operating Areas. The Operating Area Standard provides the formal definition of what an Operating Area is. Without this definition, the term "Operating Area" would carry only informal meaning and could be interpreted as an organisational unit, a project, or a task — all of which it is not.

### Rationale

Defining the Operating Area as a bounded execution context instantiated by a Commitment preserves the commitment-centric architecture. Operating Areas are not permanent organisational structures. They do not exist outside of a Commitment. They are scoped, temporary, and purposeful. The word "bounded" is deliberate: it establishes the principle of absolute execution boundaries that prevents scope expansion at runtime.

### Impact

All future standards, AIOS design, Office standards, and governance documents shall treat Operating Areas as instantiated execution contexts, not as standing organisational units. An Operating Area that is not instantiated by a Commitment does not exist. The Operating Area Standard governs their definition, and the Commitment Standard governs their ownership.

