## AD-060

**IDENTIFIER:** AD-060
**TITLE:** Office Fulfilment Models Are Implementation Concerns Outside the Office Standard
**STATUS:** Approved — Awaiting Execute
**REGISTERED:** 2026-07-03
**INITIATIVE:** INI-003
**OWNER:** Black Umbrella Governance
**SOURCE PROPOSAL:** OFFICE_STANDARD_PROPOSAL_2026-07-03_V1.md
**TEMPORARY REFERENCE:** AD-L

### Decision Statement

The Office Standard is intentionally agnostic regarding fulfilment. Capability may be fulfilled by one AI system, multiple AI systems, one human, multiple humans, external services, or future execution technologies. The Office remains the organisational capability provider regardless of fulfilment model. Executor discovery and selection are responsibilities of the AIOS Standard.

### Context

The governance architecture must remain stable across technology generations. If the Office Standard specified fulfilment models, every technology change would require a governance event. This would create administrative overhead without governance benefit and would couple the organisational architecture to specific technologies.

### Rationale

Fulfilment agnosticism preserves the separation between governance and implementation. The Office owns capability and declares a quality standard. How that capability is fulfilled is an implementation detail. This separation ensures the governance record remains stable even as execution technology evolves. Quality accountability remains with the Office regardless of the fulfilment mechanism.

### Impact

Office registrations shall never specify executors, AI systems, human teams, or software platforms. The Office Standard does not define executor discovery, selection, workload balancing, or runtime allocation. Those responsibilities are explicitly deferred to the AIOS Standard.

### Governance Status

- **Proposal:** OFFICE_STANDARD_PROPOSAL_2026-07-03_V1.md
- **Founder Approval:** Granted — 2026-07-03
- **Registered:** 2026-07-03
