## AD-083

**IDENTIFIER:** AD-083  
**TITLE:** Local-First Execution Principle  
**STATUS:** Approved  
**REGISTERED:** 2026-07-04  
**INITIATIVE:** INI-001
**OWNER:** Architecture & Governance  
**SOURCE:** Architectural discussion — Holdings Bridge design session  

### Decision Statement

The platform performs every operation using the least-complex execution path capable of satisfying the requested capability. Local execution is the default. External capability providers are invoked only when the requested capability cannot be satisfied locally. External execution must always be intentional and initiated by an explicit operational requirement, never as a consequence of simply operating the platform.

### Context

During the design of Holdings Bridge — the Black Umbrella Group operational orchestration layer — an explicit principle was needed to govern how the platform decides between local execution and external AI provider invocation. Without this principle, platform components could default to AI invocation for operations that do not require it, generating unnecessary token costs, latency, and external dependencies.

The Local-First Execution Principle establishes that the platform's default state is local and dormant. AI capability is a specialist instrument, not the default runtime path.

### Rationale

Local execution is faster, cheaper, more reliable, and more portable than external provider invocation. Repository operations, file operations, search, indexing, document lookup, version comparison, and dashboard rendering can all be performed locally without loss of quality. Defaulting to external invocation for these operations would make the platform operationally expensive and dependent on provider availability.

The principle is deliberately framed around capability resolution rather than a specific provider (e.g. "Anthropic API"). This ensures the decision remains valid if the external provider changes — whether to a different commercial provider, a local model, or another reasoning engine.

### Implications

1. Repository operations remain local.
2. File operations remain local.
3. Search remains local.
4. Indexing remains local.
5. Dashboard rendering remains local.
6. Document management remains local.
7. AIOS is invoked only when the requested capability requires reasoning, synthesis, generation, review, or research that cannot be satisfied locally.
8. Holdings Bridge (or any future execution orchestrator) owns the decision of which execution path is appropriate for each request.
9. Opening or running the platform must never, by itself, invoke external capability providers.
10. Every external invocation must be traceable to an explicit operational requirement.

### Boundary

This decision governs the execution path selection logic of platform orchestration components. It does not govern the internal behaviour of AIOS or any Office once invoked. Once Holdings Bridge determines that external execution is required and constructs an Artefact Package, AIOS governs the reasoning cycle.

### Impact

All Black Umbrella platform components. Holdings Bridge (primary implementation). AIOS Dashboard. All future orchestration layers. Any component that could route work to an external provider must apply this principle as its default decision rule.

### Governance Status

- **Produced By:** Architectural discussion — Architecture & Governance (ChatGPT) and Implementation Lead (Claude)
- **Approved:** 2026-07-04
- **Registered:** 2026-07-04
- **Supersedes:** None
- **Superseded By:** None

### References

- Holdings Bridge design session — 2026-07-04
- AIOS V1.0 Release (governing runtime that Holdings Bridge invokes)

