## AD-AIOS-103 — Two Companies, One Platform: Black Umbrella Owns and Operates AIOS; ZYRA Pays Under an Intercompany Agreement; Interim blackumbrella@ Identity

### Classification: Corporate/Commercial Architecture — APPROVED (Founder-ratified 2026-08-22: "lets build that then", confirming the one-platform model with ZYRA logging into Black Umbrella's AIOS) — requires Founder approval to amend

**Initiative:** INI-001 / Black Umbrella corporate structure

### Summary

Founder direction (2026-08-22): Black Umbrella and ZYRA are two separate
companies and must remain separable, especially for tax, because ZYRA will
pay Black Umbrella fees. He asked whether that requires TWO separate AIOS
systems (ZYRA pays BUG to manage ZYRA's AIOS, or ZYRA pays BUG for its own
separate system) and delegated the choice ("whichever you think is
better"), plus directed an interim `blackumbrella@zyraconnect.app` alias
until Black Umbrella has its own email/domain.

### Decision (ratified 2026-08-22)

1. **ONE AIOS platform. Black Umbrella owns and operates it. ZYRA is its
   first customer.** No second AIOS system is built.
   - Two instances would double maintenance, drift, and failure modes —
     the duplication the feature-review process exists to reject — while
     adding zero legal substance: **tax positions are created by
     contracts, invoices, and books, not by which server runs the
     software.** An accountant can charge for one managed platform
     exactly as well as for two.
   - AIOS already separates the entities where it matters: per-company
     ledgers (company_id, Accounting Phase 1), entity-split design
     (OA-AIOS-347), entity-grouped workforce (AD-AIOS-101), separated
     cloud projects and credentials (AD-AIOS-102). The platform is ONE
     system that KEEPS TWO COMPANIES' BOOKS — which is precisely what an
     operating company charging a product company needs.

2. **Commercial form (paper it at incorporation, not before):** a single
   **Platform & Services Agreement** — Black Umbrella provides the AIOS
   platform *and its operation* to ZYRA as a managed service for a
   recurring fee. This merges the Founder's two options: the licence
   character of "ZYRA pays for its AIOS" folded into the managed-service
   character of "BUG manages ZYRA's AIOS". One agreement, one recurring
   invoice, one ledger flow (already representable in Finance today).
   - IP intent to record at incorporation: AIOS code and the BUG
     governance estate are assigned to Black Umbrella; the ZYRA app
     codebase is assigned to ZYRA.
   - **Caveat, stated plainly: Fable is not an accountant or tax
     adviser.** Connected-company charges must be commercially
     defensible and properly papered; fee level, VAT treatment, and
     whether BUG sits as parent or sibling are set WITH an accountant at
     incorporation. Nothing is tax-effective before either company
     exists — today's job is only to keep the streams cleanly separable
     so day-one invoicing is trivial.

3. **Interim Black Umbrella identity (Founder-directed, built 2026-08-22):**
   `blackumbrella@zyraconnect.app` — alias #9 on admin@, Gmail label
   "Black Umbrella" (black), filter `deliveredto:blackumbrella@...` →
   label; Email Log Search: Delivered to Gmail mailbox, 0.75s. Standing
   rule extended: **new BUG-side signups use blackumbrella@; new
   ZYRA-side development signups use dev@; admin@ stays unpublished.**
   Which company a service belongs to is visible at a glance — even
   while both ride one domain.

4. **Known compromise, accepted knowingly:** a Black Umbrella identity on
   the zyraconnect.app domain is interim by definition. At BUG
   incorporation + own domain: BUG mail moves to its domain, and
   services signed up on blackumbrella@ re-point **one reviewed account
   at a time** (same discipline as dev@ migrations, AD-AIOS-102 §4).
   Separate BUG GitHub org: created by the Founder's hands when the
   governance repo migration is due (account creation is always
   Founder-hands); until then the ZYRA-APP-LAB placement stays a
   recorded mix (AD-AIOS-102 §6).

5. **What changes in AIOS day-to-day: nothing.** One platform, built as
   before; entity separation continues in data and design; the Finance
   ledger carries the intercompany flow the day it starts.

### Consequences

ZYRA_EMAIL moves to V2 (9 aliases). ACCESS register alias rows updated.
This AD anchors the incorporation-day checklist (agreement, IP
assignment, fee setting with accountant). Build records: OA-AIOS-350
(interim identity), OA-AIOS-351 (entity-scoped access — the customer
model built into the platform itself, same day).

